us:var(--radius);padding:20px;margin-bottom:20px;position:relative;overflow:hidden}.story-label{font-size:.72em;font-weight:600;text-transform:uppercase;letter-spacing:1.2px;color:var(--purple);margin-bottom:3px}.story-title{font-size:1.05em;font-weight:700;margin-bottom:8px;line-height:1.4;color:var(--text)}.story-text{font-size:.85em;line-height:1.7;color:var(--text-sec);margin-bottom:12px}.story-moral{font-size:.82em;font-style:italic;color:var(--emerald);padding:10px 14px;background:rgba(52,211,153,.06);border-radius:8px;border-left:3px solid var(--emerald)}.section-label{display:inline-block;font-size:.72em;font-weight:700;text-transform:uppercase;letter-spacing:1.5px;padding:4px 14px;border-radius:100px;margin:28px 0 4px}.part1{color:#60a5fa;background:rgba(96,165,250,.1)}.part2{color:var(--purple);background:rgba(167,139,250,.1)}.part3{color:var(--emerald);background:rgba(52,211,153,.1)}.part4{color:#f97316;background:rgba(249,115,22,.1)}.section-divider{height:1px;background:var(--border);margin:32px 0}.quote-box{background:linear-gradient(135deg,rgba(167,139,250,.08),rgba(52,211,153,.04));border:1px solid rgba(167,139,250,.15);border-radius:var(--radius);padding:20px;margin-bottom:20px}.quote{font-size:1em;font-weight:500;font-style:italic;color:var(--text);margin-bottom:6px}.attribution{font-size:.82em;color:var(--text-muted)}.q-card{background:var(--bg-card);border:1px solid var(--border);border-radius:var(--radius);padding:20px;margin-bottom:16px}.q-text{font-weight:600;font-size:.92em;margin-bottom:12px;line-height:1.5}.q-opts{display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:8px}.q-opt{padding:10px 14px;border-radius:8px;border:1px solid var(--border);cursor:pointer;font-size:.85em;color:var(--text-sec);transition:all .2s}.q-opt:hover{background:rgba(167,139,250,.08);border-color:rgba(167,139,250,.3)}.q-opt.correct{background:rgba(52,211,153,.15);border-color:var(--emerald);color:var(--emerald)}.q-opt.wrong{background:rgba(239,68,68,.15);border-color:var(--red);color:var(--red)}.q-opt.disabled{pointer-events:none;opacity:.6}.q-soln{display:none;margin-top:12px;padding:12px;background:rgba(167,139,250,.06);border-radius:8px;font-size:.82em;color:var(--text-sec);line-height:1.6}.q-opt.correct~.q-soln,.q-opt.wrong~.q-soln{display:block}.q-soln.show{display:block}</style></head><body><nav class="nav"><div class="nav-inner"><a href="index.html"><span class="brand-text">vlymbooq</span></a><div class="nav-links"><a href="index.html">Home</a><a href="math.html">Math Map</a><a href="physics.html">Physics Map</a><a href="chemistry.html">Chemistry</a></div></div></nav><div class="container"><div class="lesson-body"><h2>Coordination Chemistry — Ligands, Werner Theory, Crystal Field Theory &amp; Isomerism</h2><p>Coordination chemistry studies compounds formed between metal ions and ligands (molecules or ions that donate electron pairs). <strong>Werner's coordination theory</strong> (1893) proposed that metals have two types of valency: primary (ionic, corresponding to oxidation state) and secondary (coordination number, spatial arrangement). Modern theory: the metal cation accepts electron pairs from ligands via coordinate covalent bonds. The <strong>coordination entity</strong> [MLₙ] consists of a central metal ion M surrounded by n ligands L. The <strong>coordination number</strong> is the number of ligand donor atoms directly bonded to the metal. Common geometries: linear (CN = 2), tetrahedral (CN = 4), square planar (CN = 4), octahedral (CN = 6). Ligands are classified by denticity: <strong>monodentate</strong> (one donor atom, e.g., NH₃, Cl⁻, H₂O), <strong>bidentate</strong> (two donor atoms, e.g., en = ethylenediamine, ox = oxalate), <strong>polydentate</strong> (multiple donor atoms, e.g., EDTA is hexadentate). <strong>Chelation</strong>: when a polydentate ligand forms a ring with the metal, producing a more stable complex (the chelate effect). <strong>Isomerism</strong> in coordination compounds: structural isomers (different bonds) and stereoisomers (same bonds, different spatial arrangement). <strong>Crystal field theory</strong> explains the splitting of d-orbitals in the presence of ligands, giving rise to color and magnetic properties.</p><div class="quote-box"><div class="quote">"Coordination chemistry bridges inorganic chemistry with biochemistry — from hemoglobin to chlorophyll, life depends on metal-ligand complexes."</div><div class="attribution">— Adapted from Stephen J. Lippard</div></div><div class="section-label part1">Part 1</div><h2>🔗 Werner's Theory and Bonding in Coordination Compounds</h2><div class="story-card"><div class="story-label">Foundations</div><div class="story-title">Werner's Postulates, Primary and Secondary Valency, Effective Atomic Number, and Valence Bond Theory</div><div class="story-text"><strong>Werner's coordination theory</strong> (Nobel Prize 1913) revolutionized inorganic chemistry. Key postulates: (1) Metals exhibit two types of valency — <strong>primary (ionizable)</strong> corresponding to oxidation state, and <strong>secondary (non-ionizable)</strong> corresponding to coordination number. (2) Secondary valencies are directed in space — giving the geometry of the complex. (3) Each metal has a fixed coordination number (typically 4 or 6). For example, in CoCl₃·6NH₃ (hexaamminecobalt(III) chloride): the primary valency of Co is 3 (balanced by three Cl⁻), and the secondary valency is 6 (satisfied by six NH₃ ligands). The formula is [Co(NH₃)₆]Cl₃ — three Cl⁻ are ionic (precipitated by Ag⁺), six NH₃ are coordinated. <strong>Effective atomic number (EAN)</strong>: the total number of electrons around the central metal (metal electrons + donated electrons). EAN often equals the atomic number of the next noble gas (the 18- or 16-electron rule). Example: [Fe(CN)₆]⁴⁻ — Fe²⁺ has 24e⁻, six CN⁻ donate 12e⁻, EAN = 36 = Kr. <strong>Valence bond theory</strong> (Pauling, Linus): hybrid orbitals of the metal accept lone pairs from ligands. Octahedral: d²sp³ (inner orbital) or sp³d² (outer orbital). Tetrahedral: sp³. Square planar: dsp² (e.g., [Ni(CN)₄]²⁻).</div><div class="story-moral">Werner's concept of primary and secondary valency laid the foundation for modern coordination chemistry; the EAN rule helps predict the stability of metal complexes.</div></div><div class="section-label part2">Part 2</div><h2>🧩 Nomenclature and Isomerism</h2><div class="story-card"><div class="story-label">Identification</div><div class="story-title">IUPAC Nomenclature Rules, Structural Isomerism, and Stereoisomerism in Coordination Compounds</div><div class="story-text"><strong>IUPAC nomenclature</strong>: (1) Name the ligands in alphabetical order (ignoring prefixes like di-, tri-). Anionic ligands end in -o (Cl⁻ → chloro, CN⁻ → cyano, OH⁻ → hydroxo). Neutral ligands use the molecule name (NH₃ → ammine, H₂O → aqua, CO → carbonyl). (2) Name the central metal; if the complex is an anion, add -ate (e.g., cobalt → cobaltate). (3) Indicate the oxidation state in Roman numerals in parentheses. Example: [Co(NH₃)₅Cl]Cl₂ → pentaamminechloridocobalt(III) chloride. <strong>Structural isomerism</strong>: (a) <strong>Ionization isomerism</strong>: different ions in solution (e.g., [Co(NH₃)₅Br]SO₄ vs [Co(NH₃)₅SO₄]Br). (b) <strong>Hydrate (solvate) isomerism</strong>: water as coordinated vs lattice water (e.g., [Cr(H₂O)₆]Cl₃ (violet) vs [Cr(H₂O)₅Cl]Cl₂·H₂O (green)). (c) <strong>Linkage isomerism</strong>: ambidentate ligand can bind through different atoms (e.g., NO₂⁻: nitro (N-bonded) vs nitrito (O-bonded)). (d) <strong>Coordination isomerism</strong>: exchange of ligands between cationic and anionic complexes. <strong>Stereoisomerism</strong>: (a) <strong>Geometrical isomerism</strong> — in square planar [Pt(NH₃)₂Cl₂]: cis (same side, polar) and trans (opposite sides, non-polar). In octahedral [Co(NH₃)₄Cl₂]⁺: cis and trans. (b) <strong>Optical isomerism</strong>: non-superimposable mirror images (chiral complexes) — e.g., [Co(en)₃]³⁺ has Δ (right-handed) and Λ (left-handed) isomers, which rotate plane-polarized light in opposite directions.</div><div class="story-moral">Naming coordination compounds follows systematic IUPAC rules; isomerism — both structural and stereochemical — dramatically affects the physical and chemical properties of complexes.</div></div><div class="section-label part3">Part 3</div><h2>💎 Crystal Field Theory</h2><div class="story-card"><div class="story-label">Splitting</div><div class="story-title">Crystal Field Splitting, Spectrochemical Series, CFSE, Color, and Magnetic Properties</div><div class="story-text"><strong>Crystal field theory (CFT)</strong> (Bethe, 1929; van Vleck): the interaction between metal d-orbitals and the electric field of ligands causes splitting of d-orbital energies. In an <strong>octahedral field</strong>, the five d-orbitals split into two sets: t₂g (d_xy, d_yz, d_zx — lower energy) and e_g (d_z², d_x²−y² — higher energy). The energy gap is Δ₀ (or 10 Dq). The magnitude of Δ₀ depends on the ligand field strength — the <strong>spectrochemical series</strong>: I⁻ < Br⁻ < Cl⁻ < F⁻ < OH⁻ < H₂O < NH₃ < en < NO₂⁻ < CN⁻ < CO (weak to strong field). <strong>High-spin vs low-spin</strong>: for d⁴–d⁷ configurations, strong-field ligands (large Δ₀) cause electron pairing in t₂g (low-spin), while weak-field ligands (small Δ₀) give high-spin with electrons in e_g. <strong>Crystal field stabilization energy (CFSE)</strong>: the energy lowering from placing electrons in t₂g vs the barycenter. For octahedral: CFSE = [−0.4 n(t₂g) + 0.6 n(e_g)] Δ₀. <strong>Tetrahedral splitting</strong>: smaller (Δ_t = 4/9 Δ₀) and inverted — e lower, t₂ higher. <strong>Color</strong>: complexes absorb visible light corresponding to d-d transitions (Δ₀ = hν). The complementary color is observed. E.g., [Ti(H₂O)₆]³⁺ absorbs yellow-green (Δ₀ ≈ 20,300 cm⁻¹) and appears violet. <strong>Magnetic properties</strong>: unpaired electrons give paramagnetism (measured by magnetic moment μ = √[n(n+2)] BM). Low-spin complexes have fewer unpaired electrons than high-spin. <strong>Jahn-Teller distortion</strong>: non-degenerate electronic states in octahedral complexes cause elongation/compression (e.g., [Cu(H₂O)₆]²⁺ has elongated octahedral geometry due to d⁹ configuration with unevenly filled e_g orbitals).</div><div class="story-moral">Crystal field theory explains the color, magnetism, and geometry of coordination compounds through d-orbital splitting — the magnitude of splitting depends on ligand field strength.</div></div><div class="section-label part4">Part 4</div><h2>🧲 Organometallics, Bioinorganic Chemistry, and Applications</h2><div class="story-card"><div class="story-label">Applications</div><div class="story-title">Metal Carbonyls, Organometallic Compounds, Biological Metal Complexes, and Catalysis</div><div class="story-text"><strong>Metal carbonyls</strong>: complexes with CO ligands — e.g., Ni(CO)₄ (tetrahedral, 18e⁻), Fe(CO)₅ (trigonal bipyramidal), Cr(CO)₆ (octahedral). The 18-electron rule (EAN rule) predicts stability. CO is a strong-field ligand (π-acceptor) — the M−C≡O bond involves σ-donation from C to metal and π-back-donation from metal d-orbitals to CO π* orbitals, strengthening the bond. <strong>Organometallic compounds</strong>: M−C bonds — e.g., Grignard reagents (R−Mg−X), ferrocene Fe(C₅H₅)₂ (sandwich compound), Zeise's salt K[PtCl₃(C₂H₄)] (first organometallic). <strong>Bioinorganic chemistry</strong>: <strong>hemoglobin</strong> (Fe²⁺ in porphyrin ring binds O₂ reversibly), <strong>chlorophyll</strong> (Mg²⁺ in porphyrin-like ring for photosynthesis), <strong>vitamin B₁₂</strong> (Co³⁺ in corrin ring), <strong>cytochrome c</strong> (Fe in electron transport). <strong>Applications in catalysis</strong>: <strong>Wilkinson's catalyst</strong> RhCl(PPh₃)₃ for hydrogenation of alkenes, <strong>Ziegler-Natta catalyst</strong> TiCl₄/Al(Et)₃ for polymerization of ethylene, <strong>Wacker process</strong> PdCl₂/CuCl₂ for oxidation of ethylene to acetaldehyde. <strong>Magnetism in complexes</strong>: <strong>ferromagnetism</strong> (aligned moments), <strong>antiferromagnetism</strong> (opposite alignment), <strong>paramagnetism</strong> (random in no field). The <strong>spin-only formula</strong> μ = √[n(n+2)] BM works well for first-row transition metals. <strong>Stability constants</strong>: K_f = [MLₙ]/[M][L]ⁿ — stepwise formation constants (K₁, K₂, …, K_n) and overall stability constant β_n = K₁·K₂·…·K_n. The <strong>chelate effect</strong>: complexes with chelating ligands are more stable than those with similar monodentate ligands due to favorable entropy.</div><div class="story-moral">Organometallic and bioinorganic complexes demonstrate the vital role of coordination chemistry in catalysis, medicine, and life processes — from hydrogenation catalysts to hemoglobin.</div></div><div class="section-divider"></div><h2 style="margin-top:0">🎯 Practice</h2><div id="practice-qs"></div><div style="margin-top:16px"><button class="btn btn-primary" onclick="resetPractice()">🔄 Reset All</button><a href="chemistry.html" class="btn btn-success">← Back to Chemistry</a></div></div></div><script>var practiceQs=[{id:46121,text:"🔗 According to Werner's theory, the secondary valency of a metal corresponds to:",opts:[{l:"A",t:"Coordination number",c:true},{l:"B",t:"Oxidation state",c:false},{l:"C",t:"Ionization potential",c:false},{l:"D",t:"Number of ions in solution",c:false}],sol:"<strong>Secondary valency</strong> in Werner's theory corresponds to the coordination number — the number of ligand donor atoms directly bonded to the metal."},{id:46122,text:"🔗 The effective atomic number (EAN) of [Fe(CN)₆]⁴⁻ is:",opts:[{l:"A",t:"36 (Kr)",c:true},{l:"B",t:"18 (Ar)",c:false},{l:"C",t:"54 (Xe)",c:false},{l:"D",t:"46 (Pd)",c:false}],sol:"Fe²⁺ (24e⁻) + 6 CN⁻ (12e⁻) = 36e⁻ = EAN of Kr. The EAN rule predicts stable complexes often have the next noble gas configuration."},{id:46123,text:"🔗 In valence bond theory, an octahedral complex uses which hybrid orbitals for inner orbital complexes?",opts:[{l:"A",t:"d²sp³",c:true},{l:"B",t:"sp³d²",c:false},{l:"C",t:"dsp²",c:false},{l:"D",t:"sp³",c:false}],sol:"<strong>Inner orbital octahedral complexes</strong> use d²sp³ hybridization (e.g., low-spin [Co(NH₃)₆]³⁺). Outer orbital uses sp³d² (e.g., high-spin [CoF₆]³⁻)."},{id:46124,text:"🧩 The IUPAC name of K₃[Fe(CN)₆] is:",opts:[{l:"A",t:"Potassium hexacyanoferrate(III)",c:true},{l:"B",t:"Potassium hexacyanidoferrate(III)",c:false},{l:"C",t:"Tripotassium hexacyanoiron(III)",c:false},{l:"D",t:"Potassium ferricyanide",c:false}],sol:"The IUPAC name: <strong>potassium hexacyanoferrate(III)</strong> (or hexacyanidoferrate(III)). Anionic complex → -ate suffix, Fe in +3 oxidation state."},{id:46125,text:"🧩 Linkage isomerism occurs when:",opts:[{l:"A",t:"An ambidentate ligand can bind through two different atoms",c:true},{l:"B",t:"Water can be inside or outside coordination sphere",c:false},{l:"C",t:"The complex has non-superimposable mirror images",c:false},{l:"D",t:"Ligands exchange between cation and anion",c:false}],sol:"<strong>Linkage isomerism</strong> arises from ambidentate ligands (e.g., NO₂⁻, SCN⁻) that can coordinate through different donor atoms."},{id:46126,text:"🧩 Optical isomerism in coordination compounds requires:",opts:[{l:"A",t:"A chiral complex with non-superimposable mirror images",c:true},{l:"B",t:"Cis-trans arrangement of ligands",c:false},{l:"C",t:"Different ligand coordination modes",c:false},{l:"D",t:"Ionization in solution",c:false}],sol:"<strong>Optical isomers</strong> are non-superimposable mirror images (enantiomers) — e.g., Δ and Λ isomers of [Co(en)₃]³⁺."},{id:46127,text:"💎 In crystal field theory, the splitting of d-orbitals in an octahedral field produces:",opts:[{l:"A",t:"Two sets: t₂g (lower) and e_g (higher)",c:true},{l:"B",t:"Two sets: e_g (lower) and t₂g (higher)",c:false},{l:"C",t:"Three sets of degenerate orbitals",c:false},{l:"D",t:"No splitting",c:false}],sol:"In <strong>octahedral CFT</strong>, d-orbitals split into t₂g (d_xy, d_yz, d_zx — lower energy) and e_g (d_z², d_x²−y² — higher energy)."},{id:46128,text:"💎 Which ligand is at the strong-field end of the spectrochemical series?",opts:[{l:"A",t:"CO",c:true},{l:"B",t:"H₂O",c:false},{l:"C",t:"Cl⁻",c:false},{l:"D",t:"I⁻",c:false}],sol:"<strong>CO (carbonyl)</strong> is the strongest field ligand in the <strong>spectrochemical series</strong>, causing the largest Δ₀ splitting."},{id:46129,text:"💎 A low-spin d⁶ octahedral complex has how many unpaired electrons?",opts:[{l:"A",t:"0",c:true},{l:"B",t:"2",c:false},{l:"C",t:"4",c:false},{l:"D",t:"6",c:false}],sol:"<strong>Low-spin d⁶</strong> (strong field): all 6 electrons pair in t₂g (↑↓ ↑↓ ↑↓), giving 0 unpaired electrons — diamagnetic."},{id:46130,text:"🧲 The 18-electron rule in metal carbonyls predicts stability when:",opts:[{l:"A",t:"The sum of metal electrons and donated electrons is 18",c:true},{l:"B",t:"The coordination number is 18",c:false},{l:"C",t:"18 ligand atoms are present",c:false},{l:"D",t:"The metal oxidation state is +18",c:false}],sol:"The <strong>18-electron rule</strong> (EAN rule): stable organometallic complexes typically have 18 valence electrons (metal d-electrons + ligand donations)."},{id:46131,text:"🧲 Hemoglobin contains which metal ion at its active site?",opts:[{l:"A",t:"Fe²⁺",c:true},{l:"B",t:"Mg²⁺",c:false},{l:"C",t:"Co³⁺",c:false},{l:"D",t:"Cu²⁺",c:false}],sol:"<strong>Hemoglobin</strong> contains Fe²⁺ coordinated to a porphyrin ring. It reversibly binds O₂ for oxygen transport in blood."},{id:46132,text:"🧲 The chelate effect refers to:",opts:[{l:"A",t:"Higher stability of complexes with polydentate ligands vs monodentate analogs",c:true},{l:"B",t:"The color of chelate complexes",c:false},{l:"C",t:"The magnetic property of chelates",c:false},{l:"D",t:"The solubility of chelates in organic solvents",c:false}],sol:"The <strong>chelate effect</strong>: complexes with chelating ligands (like en) are more stable than similar monodentate complexes due to favorable entropy increase."}];var answered={},correctCount=0;function renderPractice(){var c=document.getElementById('practice-qs'),h='';for(var i=0;i<practiceQs.length;i++){var q=practiceQs[i],o='';for(var j=0;j<q.opts.length;j++){o+='<div class="q-opt" onclick="selectOpt('+q.id+','+j+')">'+q.opts[j].l+'. '+q.opts[j].t+'</div>';}h+='<div class="q-card" id="q-'+q.id+'"><div class="q-text">'+(i+1)+'. '+q.text+'</div><div class="q-opts">'+o+'</div><div class="q-soln" id="soln-'+q.id+'"><strong>'+getCorrectLabel(q)+'</strong><br>'+q.sol+'</div></div>';}c.innerHTML=h;}function getCorrectLabel(q){for(var i=0;i<q.opts.length;i++){if(q.opts[i].c)return q.opts[i].l+'. '+q.opts[i].t;}return '';}function selectOpt(qId,idx){if(answered[qId])return;answered[qId]=true;var q=practiceQs.filter(function(x){return x.id===qId})[0];var opts=document.querySelectorAll('#q-'+qId+' .q-opt'),isCorrect=q.opts[idx].c;for(var i=0;i<opts.length;i++)opts[i].classList.add('disabled');if(isCorrect){opts[idx].classList.add('correct');correctCount++;}else{opts[idx].classList.add('wrong');for(var i=0;i<q.opts.length;i++){if(q.opts[i].c)opts[i].classList.add('correct');}}document.getElementById('soln-'+qId).classList.add('show');}function resetPractice(){if(!confirm('Reset?'))return;answered={};correctCount=0;document.querySelectorAll('.q-opt').forEach(function(e){e.className='q-opt'});document.querySelectorAll('.q-soln').forEach(function(e){e.classList.remove('show')});renderPractice();}renderPractice();</script></body></html>